  <title>
   <narrative xml:lang="EN">Addressing Converging Crises and Unprecedented Increase in Needs of IDPs, Returnees and Their Families in Northern Central America (NCA)</narrative>
  </title>
  <description>
   <narrative xml:lang="EN">In Guatemala, El Salvador and Honduras, thousands of internally displaced persons continue to live in collective centres, shelters, informal settlements and some more live in the open. These families face inherent risks that make them extremely vulnerable. Not having access to decent housing solutions, many families have returned to their old, damaged homes, among other reasons, due to the existing risks of living in these temporary sites and having to protect their assets, which are in danger due to lack of surveillance by themselves and the authorities. Faced with this situation, this project provides for various preventive actions to prepare for an emergency due to a natural or anthropic disaster, including through strengthening the collective centres, shelters or similar sites identified in each of the three countries mentioned, their use being the last option for displaced people. Additionally, adequate protection mechanisms are being developed for women, adolescents and girls, and other vulnerable groups.</narrative>
  </description>
